tes $943K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $113K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 0\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (1/10).","financial_summary":["Summit Hotel Properties, Inc. is a small-cap real estate business with a $846M market cap on $729M of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +3% over the past three years and +5% annually across the full decade, which puts it in mature, slow-growth territory.","Returns on capital run at 3% against a 7% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $74M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 10 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 6.3\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 0.8\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Management returns a meaningful 6.4% of market cap to owners through dividends and buybacks, a strong signal of disciplined capital allocation, and insiders hold 6.6% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.5\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Piotroski score 3/9 \u2014 the fundamentals are deteriorating on multiple fronts","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.5) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Payout ratio of 200% leaves essentially no coverage if earnings wobble","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins 14% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","c